8936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088621 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088621 #edb/ref 1061 20974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088621 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1aa874fd-fdaf-5a85-95df-854b01cff7e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088621 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088622 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088622 #edb/ref 1061 20975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088622 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fc53c19e-fe36-588b-8fde-bb5c33eab305" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088622 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088623 #edb/ref 1061 20976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088623 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d2193b17-f424-59a7-88cc-dcdc12efdb4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088623 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088638 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088638 #edb/ref 1061 23298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088638 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0d5a8786-d0ae-54eb-bba0-009fae043bc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088638 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088936 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the complex number $z=3+4\\textrm{i},$ as depicted on the Argand diagram below:\n\n![](images/c8/c857172451c97c24caab685a2f52097c0ca3c83df212c46a21f4a49c3edb19ca.png)\n\nThere is a natural angle $\\theta$ that $z$ makes with the positive real axis. This angle is called the **argument** of $z.$ We often denote the argument as $\\arg(z),$ or just $\\theta.$ The numeric value of $\\arg(z)$ is given in radians, and we restrict the values to \n\n$$\n0\\leq \\arg(z) \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nIn the triangle above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.93,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. Therefore $\\arg (z) \\approx 0.93$ to two decimal places.\n\n**Caution:** When you evaluate $\\arctan \\left( \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ on your calculator, be sure that you're in radians mode, and be sure to put parentheses around the $\\dfrac{4}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088936 #edb/ref 1065 3731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088936 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f2fefd3c-b47b-5b28-88a0-1c4769eb1e11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088936 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232407 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186232408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232407 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232407 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232407 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186232408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232407 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2bdc18ae-186c-5350-9538-a2d1a6ea5f2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232408 #edb/ref 1080 "5.62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232408 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232408 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aa713718-4023-5280-813d-3aa91128a3be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233711 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186232407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233711 #edb/ref 1069 "q-275159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233711 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233711 #edb/ref 1071 "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=9-7\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233711 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c91f4f8-2fbe-5963-a53b-2aead62a5f7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233711 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/b6/b6ec4332891a4c7684be8de2ca94bf24a1d9abd5170d9fe38ad3a1346afae56a.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname